  },
  {
    provider: '<strong>Kamatera</strong>',
    price: '$4/mo',
    ram: '1GB',
    storage: '20GB SSD',
    bandwidth: '5TB',
    locations: '22'
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What is VPS hosting and how is it different from shared hosting?',
    answer: 'Think of shared hosting like a crowded apartment building — everyone shares the same resources and when your neighbor throws a party, your internet slows down. VPS is more like a condo. You get your own guaranteed slice of CPU, RAM, and storage that nobody else can touch. Better performance, more control, and nobody else can crash your site.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How much RAM do I need for my VPS?',
    answer: 'Most small business sites do great with 2-4GB. A basic WordPress site can honestly run on 1-2GB if you optimize it. Running multiple sites or an e-commerce store? Start at 4GB and go from there. The nice thing is you can usually upgrade without migrating, so start smaller and scale up when you need to.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is managed VPS worth the extra cost?',
    answer: 'Depends on how much your time is worth. If you do not have IT staff and the thought of server maintenance makes you want to quit, then yes — absolutely worth the extra $10-30 per month. Cloudways handles all the scary stuff (updates, security, backups) so you can focus on actually running your business.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I scale my VPS as my business grows?',
    answer: 'Yep, that is one of the best parts. Every decent VPS lets you add more RAM, CPU, and storage usually with just a few clicks and maybe a quick reboot. DigitalOcean and Vultr even offer load balancers if you eventually need multiple servers working together.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Which VPS provider is best for WordPress?',
    answer: 'If you want easy mode, Cloudways — one click WordPress install with server caching built in. If you are comfortable with a terminal, DigitalOcean or Vultr both have WordPress images in their marketplaces. If even that sounds like too much work, skip VPS entirely and use managed WordPress hosting like WP Engine.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need technical skills to manage a VPS?',
    answer: 'For unmanaged VPS, you need to be comfortable typing commands into a terminal. Nothing crazy, but you should know basic Linux. If that sounds scary, go with Cloudways — their dashboard handles everything visually. Or slap a control panel like cPanel or Plesk on any VPS and click your way through instead.'
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Web Hosting for Remote Businesses',
    excerpt: 'Complete guide to web hosting options for remote teams, from shared to dedicated.',
    href: '/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31-400w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Server room infrastructure'
  },
  {
    title: 'Cloud vs Traditional Hosting',
    excerpt: 'Understand the key differences to choose the right hosting type.',
    href: '/blog/cloud-vs-traditional-hosting',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa-400w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Cloud computing concept'
  },
  {
    title: 'Website Speed Optimization Guide',
    excerpt: 'Complete guide to making your website load faster.',
    href: '/tools/website-speed-optimization-guide',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1460925895917-afdab827c52f-400w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Website performance metrics dashboard'
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
    category="Hosting"
    categorySlug="tools/hosting"
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <section id="introduction">
    <p class="lead">
      I hit that wall about three years ago. My sites were growing, my shared hosting was choking, and I kept getting
      those dreaded "resource limit exceeded" emails. That is when I made the jump to VPS — and honestly, I should have
      done it sooner.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here is the thing about <strong>VPS (Virtual Private Server) hosting</strong>: it sounds intimidating, but it does
      not have to be. You get your own slice of a server with guaranteed resources that nobody else can touch. No more
      mystery slowdowns because some stranger's site on the same server went viral.
    </p>
    <p>
      I have tested pretty much every VPS provider out there at this point. Set up the same WordPress install on each
      one, ran benchmarks, dealt with their support teams, the whole thing. Some are amazing. Some are... not. This
      guide is everything I have learned about which ones are actually worth your money.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The short version:</strong> DigitalOcean is my go-to for anything development-related — their docs are
      unreal. Vultr gives you the most raw power per dollar. And if you want VPS performance without touching a command
      line, Cloudways handles all the server stuff for you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <section id="quick-picks">
    <h2>Quick Picks</h2>
    <ComparisonTable columns={quickPicksColumns} rows={quickPicksRows} highlight={0}/>

    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3 mt-6">
      <div class="bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-lg p-4">
        <span class="text-jet-stream font-semibold">Best for Developers</span>
        <p class="text-white font-bold mt-1">DigitalOcean</p>
        <p class="text-white/60 text-sm mt-2">Excellent documentation, simple API, and developer-friendly interface</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-lg p-4">
        <span class="text-jet-stream font-semibold">Best Performance/Price</span>
        <p class="text-white font-bold mt-1">Vultr</p>
        <p class="text-white/60 text-sm mt-2">High-performance NVMe storage with competitive pricing</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-lg p-4">
        <span class="text-jet-stream font-semibold">Best Managed VPS</span>
        <p class="text-white font-bold mt-1">Cloudways</p>
        <p class="text-white/60 text-sm mt-2">User-friendly dashboard with automatic backups and updates</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- VPS vs Shared vs Dedicated -->
  <section id="vps-vs-shared-vs-dedicated">
    <h2>VPS vs Shared vs Dedicated Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      Quick refresher on what you are actually choosing between, because the hosting industry loves making this
      confusing:
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
      <div class="bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-lg p-4">
        <h4 class="text-white font-semibold">Shared Hosting ($3-15/month)</h4>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-2">Think apartment building. You and a bunch of strangers share the same
          server. Cheap, sure, but when your neighbor throws a party (traffic spike), everyone suffers. Good for small
          blogs and sites that do not make money yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-jet-stream/20 border border-jet-stream/40 rounded-lg p-4">
        <h4 class="text-white font-semibold">VPS Hosting ($4-100/month)</h4>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-2">Think condo. You get your own guaranteed space with walls nobody else can
          breach. The building is still shared technically, but your resources are yours. This is where most growing
          businesses should be.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-lg p-4">
        <h4 class="text-white font-semibold">Dedicated Hosting ($100-500+/month)</h4>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-2">Think house. The entire physical machine is yours. Maximum everything, but
          also maximum cost and you need to know what you are doing. Most businesses never actually need this.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      If your business is making anywhere from $5,000 to $100,000 a year, VPS is almost certainly the right call. Enough
      power to grow, not so expensive that it eats your profits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Top 8 VPS Hosting Providers -->
  <section id="top-vps-hosting-providers">
    <h2>Top 8 VPS Hosting Providers</h2>

    <!-- DigitalOcean -->
    <div id="digitalocean" class="mt-8 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best for Developers</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">DigitalOcean</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$4/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        This is where I send everyone who is even slightly technical. DigitalOcean basically invented the "easy VPS"
        category with their Droplets. What really sets them apart is the documentation — I am not exaggerating when I
        say their tutorials have taught me more than some paid courses. Whatever obscure thing you are trying to do,
        someone at DO has written a step-by-step guide for it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ Tutorials so good they spoil you for other providers</li>
            <li>+ Dashboard is clean and actually makes sense</li>
            <li>+ Pricing is what they say it is — no weird surprises</li>
            <li>+ API is a joy to work with if you automate things</li>
            <li>+ One-click apps save hours of setup time</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- No phone support — tickets only</li>
            <li>- You need to know your way around a terminal</li>
            <li>- Windows server options are limited</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/digitalocean"
            text="Get $200 Free Credit"
            subtext="60-day free trial"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Vultr -->
    <div id="vultr" class="mt-6 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best Performance/Price</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">Vultr</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$2.50/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        If you are purely chasing performance per dollar, Vultr wins. They have 32 data centers globally (more than
        anyone else I have tested), and their NVMe storage is crazy fast. Their high-frequency compute instances
        especially — I have run benchmarks where they beat servers costing twice as much.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ More locations than anyone — 32 data centers worldwide</li>
            <li>+ The price-to-performance ratio is ridiculous</li>
            <li>+ NVMe storage on everything, even cheap plans</li>
            <li>+ Bare metal options if you really need the power</li>
            <li>+ Actually supports Windows and BSD properly</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- Dashboard feels a bit rough compared to DO</li>
            <li>- Documentation is okay but not amazing</li>
            <li>- Support is hit or miss depending on who you get</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/vultr"
            text="Get $100 Free Credit"
            subtext="30-day free trial"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Linode -->
    <div id="linode" class="mt-6 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best Customer Support</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">Linode (Akamai)</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$5/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        Linode has been doing VPS hosting since 2003 — they were around before most of their competitors even existed.
        Akamai bought them recently, which sounds corporate but actually means better networking infrastructure. The
        real standout though is their support. You can actually call them and talk to a human. In 2024. I know,
        shocking.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ 24/7 human support — phone AND tickets</li>
            <li>+ Two decades of not randomly going down</li>
            <li>+ Pricing that actually makes sense</li>
            <li>+ Rock-solid uptime you can rely on</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- Not as many one-click apps as the others</li>
            <li>- Dashboard is functional but dated looking</li>
            <li>- Only 11 locations worldwide</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/linode"
            text="Get $100 Free Credit"
            subtext="60-day free trial"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- AWS Lightsail -->
    <div id="aws-lightsail" class="mt-6 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best for AWS Ecosystem</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">AWS Lightsail</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$3.50/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        Amazon finally figured out that not everyone wants to become a certified cloud architect just to spin up a
        server. Lightsail is their attempt at simple VPS — fixed prices you can actually understand, but with the door
        to all those fancy AWS services if you ever need them. If you are already in the AWS world, this makes a lot of
        sense.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ Fixed pricing that will not surprise you at month end</li>
            <li>+ Plug right into S3, RDS, whatever else you need</li>
            <li>+ Amazon's infrastructure in 18 regions</li>
            <li>+ Easy automated backups</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- AWS console will still confuse the heck out of you</li>
            <li>- Want real support? That will cost extra</li>
            <li>- Community is smaller than DO or Vultr</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/aws-lightsail"
            text="Try Lightsail Free"
            subtext="First month free (750 hours)"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Hostinger VPS -->
    <div id="hostinger-vps" class="mt-6 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best Budget Option</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">Hostinger VPS</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$3.99/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        If budget is tight and you just need something that works, Hostinger is surprisingly solid. I was skeptical at
        first — prices that low usually mean corners were cut somewhere. But they have genuinely improved, especially
        their new control panel. Is it as polished as DigitalOcean? No. But for under four bucks a month, it gets the
        job done.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ Hard to argue with these prices</li>
            <li>+ hPanel has gotten legitimately good</li>
            <li>+ Surprisingly decent performance</li>
            <li>+ Live chat actually responds quickly</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- Only 8 data center locations</li>
            <li>- Power users will find limitations</li>
            <li>- Renewal pricing is significantly higher</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/hostinger"
            text="Get 75% Off VPS"
            subtext="Limited time offer"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- A2 Hosting -->
    <div id="a2-hosting" class="mt-6 bg-white/5 border border-white/10 rounded-xl p-6">
      <div class="flex items-start justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div>
          <span class="text-xs text-jet-stream bg-jet-stream/20 px-2 py-1 rounded">Best Managed Unmanaged</span>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-2">A2 Hosting</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="text-right">
          <p class="text-white/60 text-sm">Starting at</p>
          <p class="text-2xl font-bold text-jet-stream">$7.99/month</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-white/80 mt-4">
        A2 sits in this interesting middle ground. Their Turbo servers run on LiteSpeed which is genuinely fast, and you
        can get varying levels of management depending on how hands-on you want to be. Good option if "I want a fast
        server but I do not want to become a sysadmin" describes your situation.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 mt-4">
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">What I Like</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>+ Turbo servers are legitimately quick</li>
            <li>+ Pick your management level</li>
            <li>+ They will migrate your site for free</li>
            <li>+ Money-back any time, not just 30 days</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h4 class="text-white font-semibold text-sm mb-2">The Downsides</h4>
          <ul class="text-white/70 text-sm space-y-1">
            <li>- Costs more than the pure budget options</li>
            <li>- Only 4 data centers worldwide</li>
            <li>- Checkout process is upsell city</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-white/10">
        <AffiliateButton
            href="/go/a2hosting"
